Abstract
Shaping metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) powders into formed bodies plays a crucial role in opening up the excellent properties of MOFs to a broad range of applications. Gallate-based MOFs, termed as M-gallate $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Co},\mathbf{Mg},\mathbf{Ni})$ , have shown excellent performance for adsorption separation of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons. However, the industrial applications of MOF powders will inevitably confront problems of high pressure drop, pipe blockage, and dust pollution. Herein, we use hydroxypropyl cellulose (HPC) as a binder to produce gallate-based MOFs pellets. The crystal structure of the well-shaped materials after molding remained intact, and the surface area of the materials hardly decreases after shaping. Adsorption isotherms of $\mathrm{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons including ethylene, ethane and acetylene on the activated powders and pellets of M-gallate were recorded and compared with the outperformers. The shaped pellets were also examined by breakthrough experiments on the fixed-bed separation of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (1:99, v/v) and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ (50:50, v/v) gas mixtures. These results proved that M-gallate pellets was promising candidates for the practical industrial realization of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons separation.
Keywords: Metal-organic framework; Shaping; Adsorption Separation; $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons; Gallate
## 1. Introduction
The efficient separations of $\mathrm{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbon molecules are of fundamental industrial importance[1, 2]. Ethane is the primary raw materials for ethylene production, and both acetylene and ethylene are principal feedstock for various industrial and consumer products[3]. The compact molecular sizes and very low relative volatilities make the separation of $\mathrm{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbon mixtures one of the most challenging processes in the petrochemical industry[4]. Typically, these separations are achieved by using energyintensive fractional distillation that needs simultaneously both low temperatures and high pressures[5-7]. Extensive efforts to develop alternative separation methods and technologies for a more efficient separation of $\mathrm{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons at a higher temperature and standard atmospheric pressure have focused on membrane separation, extractive distillation, and adsorption. Among these approaches, the adsorption process based on porous solid adsorbents have been recognized as being the most promising alternative. In the early $20^{\mathrm{th}}$ century, the industrial adsorbents used were mainly activated carbon and silica gel[8-10]. Following on from those, molecular sieves saw large-scale industrial applications in the field of gas adsorption separation[11-13], which greatly promoted the processing of fine chemicals, energy-producing chemicals and petrochemicals. However, the composition of small simple inorganic molecules limited the tunability and designability of their structural dimension and functional properties. Hence, it is imperative to explore new porous materials for a more efficient industrial adsorption separation.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are highly ordered crystalline materials comprised of metal clusters/metal ions interconnected via organic ligands[14]. MOFs have advanced properties over other porous materials and their use has become a hot spot across an extensive range of scientific research fields, for example biomedicine, catalysis and chemical sensing, due to their high surface area, structural tunability, and abundant functionality. Among these applications, separation and purification have remained at the forefront of research, especially for light hydrocarbons. By replacing metal nodes and ligands, MOFs can accurately distinguish between similar substances having only minor differences, which are difficult to separate without adequate separation selectivity using traditional adsorbents. Over the past decade, MOFs have been widely investigated for use in such diverse endeavors as gas storage and separation, such as $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation[15-20], $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture[21], adsorption separation of $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins[22] and so on[23-27]. However, the practical applications of these have fallen far behind academic research. Therefore, research into the feasibility of synthesizing and applying MOFs for potential industrial applications have been of great interest.
Typically, the usage of MOF materials in powder from may face difficulties such as dustiness, pressure drop across fixed-bed, pipe blockage, quality reduction as well as challenges in their transportation and handling, all of which have hindered further industrial applications of MOFs[28-31]. Molding MOFs into application-oriented forms is an indispensable part of the industrialization of metal-organic framework materials. Previous research has shown that post-synthesis operations (an additional process performed on the pre-formed crystalline powder) can shape MOFs into various forms[31]. The molded MOFs need to retain their porosity and functionality while also possessing sufficient mechanical strength and chemical stability as well as having resistance to abrasion, in order to meet the requirements for a given process. However, unlike organic polymers, MOF materials are non-thermoplastic and solvent-insoluble crystalline powders[32], meaning that conventional solvent or melt processing techniques are not suitable[33]. Currently, it has been reported that the MOF powder can be formed into extrudates or pellets by pressurization or adding a binder[34-36]. Previous practices have also demonstrated the feasibility and advantages of mechanical compression[37-40]. However, such pressurization often results in a partial collapse of pore structures or a significant loss of crystallinity. Adding a binder such as a polymer during the shaping process can help avoid the aforementioned problems. In addition, if the binder added to the process contains functional groups that are not contained in the MOFs material, it can generate different properties to the molded composite[41]. However, the presence of such a binder may also result in a decrease in adsorption capacity and separation selectivity[30, 34]. For example, MIL-53 (Al) pellets formed by adding a $15\mathrm{wt\%}$ PVA aqueous solution have a reduction in the specific surface area by $32\%$ compared with the powder[37]. In forming Zr-MOF by using sucrose, the end product lost up to $34.8\%$ of its hydrogen storage capacity[30]. Overall, an ideal adhesive should possess the properties of strong adhesion, moderate molding conditions, and good performance to ensure excellent characteristics of the MOFs[42]. In response to the challenges of the synthetic MOF material forming process, the appropriate binder and molding process should be selected to achieve well-shaped bodies and to make MOFs materials more suitable for industrialization and
commercialization.
In this work, we used hydroxypropyl cellulose (HPC) as a binder to prepare a gallatebased MOFs molding material retaining an uptake capacity of up to $90\%$ . The potential of using shaped pellets for industrial adsorption separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ was investigated by single-component adsorption isotherms and breakthrough experiments.
## 2. Experiment
## 2.1 Materials
Magnesium chloride $(\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ , $99\%$ ) was provided by Alfa Aesar Co. (USA). Cobalt chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(CoCl_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $99\%$ ), nickel chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99\%$ ), gallic acid monohydrate (3,4,5-trihydroxybenzoic acid monohydrate, ${\mathrm{C}}_{7}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}{\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ , $98\%$ ) and hydroxypropyl cellulose ( $99\%$ , M.W.100,000) were purchased from SigmaAldrich Co. (USA). All chemicals were directly used without further purification. Highpurity gases including $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}(99.99\%),C_{2}H_{4}(99.99\%),C_{2}H_{6}(99.99\%),C_{2}H_{7}(99.99\%),C O_{2}(99.999\%)}$ , He $(99.999\%)$ and mixed gases of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathrm{\C_{2}H_{6}}/=50/50$ and $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{\DeltaC}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}{=\mathrm{\Omega}1/99}$ were provided by Jingong Co., Ltd. (China).
## 2.2 Preparation of MOFs pellets
The synthesis of M-gallate $(\mathrm{M}{=}\mathrm{Ni}$ , Co, Mg) was carried out as previously reported method[43]. $\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ $\mathrm{50~mmol)}$ and gallic acid monohydrate ( $\mathrm{100~mmol}$ ) were mixed into $250~\mathrm{mL}$ KOH aqueous solution ( $_{0.16\mathrm{M}}$ for Ni, Co-gallate, $0.5{\bf M}$ for Mg-gallate) in a flask. Then the mixture was heated and refluxed at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ambient pressure for $24\mathrm{h}$ with continuous stirring. After cooling down at room temperature, the product was collected and washed with $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ethanol successively.
M-gallate pellets were prepared using hydroxypropyl cellulose (HPC) as the binder. 1g M-gallate powder was mixed uniformly with $50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{HPC}$ , and then $0.6\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added into the powder mixture by small amounts for several times while grinding. The semi-dry paste was formed into pellets of $1{-}2~\mathrm{mm}$ size by extrusion molding.
## 2.3 Characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the samples were recorded using a Panalytical XPert X-ray diffractometer $(\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{Ka}\lambda{=}1.540598\mathring\mathrm{A})$ at $40\mathrm{kV}$ . The data were collected in the range of $5\mathrm{-}60^{\circ}$ (2θ angle range) with a step size of $0.02^{\circ}$ . Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out in a TA-Q500 TGA instrument (TA
Instruments Corp., USA) from $50{-}700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in nitrogen atmosphere at a constant rate of $10~\mathrm{{^{\circ}C/m i n}}$ . The surface structure of M-gallate powder and pellets were analyzed by Hitachi S-3700N scanning electron microscope (SEM) at an accelerating voltage of 10 kV. The BET surface area of M-gallate powder and pellets were characterized by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ . The adsorption-desorption isotherms were both obtained on a Micromeritics ASAP 2460 (Micromeritics Instrument Corp., USA). Each sample was degassed at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under ultrahigh vacuum before measurement.
## 2.4 Gas adsorption isotherms
The sample of M-gallate powder and pellet was degassed at $120{^\circ\mathrm{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under ultrahigh vacuum, then the single-component isotherms for hydrocarbon gases (acetylene, ethylene, and ethane) at $273\mathrm{K}$ and $298\mathrm{K}$ were measured on a Micromeritics ASAP 2460 gas adsorption analyzer. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were performed at 195 K using the same equipment on activated powder and pellets.
## 2.5 Breakthrough experiments
The M-gallate powder and pellets were first activated under an ultra-high vacuum at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ before tests. Breakthrough experiments of binary $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures were conducted in a stainless-steel column ( $50\ \mathrm{mm}\times10\ \mathrm{mm}\ \mathrm{ID})$ , containing activated adsorbent (Ni-pellet: $2.58\ {\mathrm{g}};{\mathrm{Mg}}.$ -pellet: $2.18\mathrm{g}$ ; Co-pellet: $2.53\mathrm{g}$ ). The column was first purged with a helium gas flow for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the packed sample. The mixed gas of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ $(50/50,\mathrm{\v/v})$ was introduced at 273K with a constant flow rate of $1.0\:\mathrm{mL/min}$ . The outlet gas from the column was monitored by a GC-8A gas chromatography (SHIMADZU, Japan) with a flame ionization detector (FID). After each breakthrough experiment, the sample was regenerated with a helium flow for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The breakthrough experiment of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (1/99, v/v) mixture is conducted at $298\mathrm{K}$ using the same equipment.
## 3.  Result and discussion
## 3.1 M-gallate synthesis and pellet forming
For the shaping of inorganic materials, the process is usually carried out by calcining at relatively high temperatures to obtain high mechanical strength. However, the organic linkers contained in MOFs materials are not of high thermal-stability and should avoid such high-temperature treatments. Hence, organic binders can be considered for the shaping of MOFs. Plasticizers are additives that can improve the rheological behavior of the paste during the molding process, including polymers and different types of cellulose. Organic plasticizers have been reportedly used as binders in the shaping of MOFs to enhance the mechanical stability of the MOF pellets. Herein, three gallate-based MOFs materials were shaped into pellets using HPC as the optimal binder. The structure of HPC is shown in Fig. S1. The photograph of M-gallate powder and pellets with a diameter of $1{-}2~\mathrm{mm}$ molded by a laboratory-made granulator (Fig. S2) were shown in Fig. 1a. Microstructural of the M-gallate powder and pellet can be observed from the SEM images (Fig. 1b and c). Binders can maintain the integrity of the shaped body after drying. In granulation, liquid bridges formed between wetted particles provide the major binding force and smaller particles adhered to one another via liquid bridges to produce large particles as shown in Fig. 1c. Liquid bridges subsequently evolve into strong solid bridges during the drying treatment. It can be seen that HPC binder plays an important role as the strong bridges tightly holding MOFs crystals together, while the crystal structure of MOFs remains intact. Benefiting from abundant hydroxyl groups that enable HPC to possess strong adhesive force, HPC showed prominent performance in enhancing agglomeration properties of the particles in MOFs and improving the mechanical stability of the shaped pellets. To further confirm the structure of MOFs pellets, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of powder and pellet samples were shown in Fig. 2a. The XRD curves showed that the M-gallate powder and pellet have a similar diffraction pattern, indicating the crystal structure of M-gallate is rarely affected by the molding process. The thermogravimetric analysis curves of M-gallate samples before and after shaping (Fig. 2c and S4) showed similar weight losses. The decomposition temperature of both powder and pellet samples was about $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The weight loss before this temperature almost coincided, which resulted from the loss of crystal water. HPC binder can withstand the high temperature from 250 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and weight loss after $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was attributed to its decomposition. This demonstrated that the molding process did not compromise its thermal stability.
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $195\mathrm{K}$ of the powder and shaped M-gallate samples are observed to compare the effect of shaping on the surface area $(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}})$ of the M-gallate. The reductions of $\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ in three shaped materials ( $\mathbf{Mg}$ -gallate, Co-gallate, Ni-gallate) were calculated to be $12.7\%$ , $2.8\%$ , and $6.6\%$ , respectively, corresponding to the amount of binder added. It was found that $\mathrm{\DeltaS_{BET}}$ of Ni-gallate calculated from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 2c) slightly decreased from $455~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ to $425\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ ， suggesting that addition of HPC has minor influence on the surface area.
To evaluate the performance of shaped pellets in mechanical stability, abrasion tests were performed, in which 200 pellets were tumbled at a speed of 30rpm for $600\ \mathrm{min}$ . As expected, the cumulative percentage of breakage was below $5\%$ . Moreover, we have measured the crushing resistance of the shaped pellets (Table 1). Results demonstrate that the $\mathrm{Co}$ and Ni-gallate pellets have better mechanical strength than spherical zeolite 13X.
## 3.2 Gas adsorption analysis
at $298\mathrm{K}$ on three M-gallate pellets and powders. (a) Co-gallate powder and pellet. (b) $\mathbf{Mg}$ -gallate powder and pellet. (c) Ni-gallate powder and pellet. (grey: powder; purple: Co-gallate pellets; red: $\mathbf{Mg}$ -gallate pellets; green: Ni-gallate pellets).
To better investigate the separation performance of the pellets, the adsorption selectivity of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ $(50{:}50,\mathrm{v/v})$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (1:99, v/v) gas mixture at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar was calculated using the Ideal Adsorbed Solution Theory (IAST). We correlate the adsorption isotherm of three $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons on pellet samples at $298\mathrm{K}$ based on the dual-site Langmuir-Freundlich (DSLF) model, and the fitting equation parameters were summarized in Table S1. We compared the IAST selectivity of shaped pellets with pure powder and other popular porous materials. As seen in Fig. 4a, the calculated IAST selectivity of Ni-gallate pellet for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (1:99, v/v) gas mixture is higher than that of the powder sample, attributed to the increased gap between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms. Although the calculated IAST selectivity of $\mathrm{Co}$ -gallate and $\operatorname{Mg}.$ -gallate pellet samples decrease when compared with the pure powder, it is still higher than most of well-known powder MOFs at similar conditions (Fig. 4a). Meanwhile, the IAST selectivity of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ (50:50, v/v) for $\mathrm{Co}$ -gallate, $\mathbf{Mg}.$ -gallate, and Ni-gallate pellets were 47.0, 31.8, and 13.5, respectively, proving an excellent $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation when compared with most of powder porous materials, such as zeolite 13X, zeolite 5A and MgMOF-74 (Fig. 4b). These data revealed that the shaped pellets possess a remarkable performance for adsorption separation of both $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ (50:50, v/v) and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (1:99, v/v) gas mixture.
In industrial applications, energy consumption for the regeneration process should also be considered as a major challenge. In order to investigate the interactions between the adsorbents and adsorbates, the isosteric heats of adsorption $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\right)$ were calculated by using virial and Clausius–Clapeyron equation (Fig. S8). The hierarchy of the calculated $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ for $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons is $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ , which is consistent with powder samples. The discrepancy between isosteric heat for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ exhibited the different binding affinity of M-gallate pellets towards $\mathrm{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons. The isosteric heats for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ of three molded materials at low loading were measured to be 51, 53 and $40\mathrm{kJ/mol}(\mathrm{Table}2)$ , which were lower than other MOF materials with open metal sites, indicating that M-gallate materials are easily activated and regenerated.
## 3.3 Separation of $C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}$ $(50;50,\nu/\nu)$ and $C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}$ (1:99, v/v) mixtures
According to previous research, granulation will not only have an impact on static adsorption but also can result in great resistance to dynamic mass transfer, which should also be considered when it comes to industrial applications. Therefore, breakthrough tests of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ (50:50, v/v) and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (1:99, v/v) mixtures were conducted to validate the feasibility of using M-gallate pellets under practical conditions for gas separation.
In the case of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}\left(50{:}50,\mathrm{v/v}\right)$ breakthrough experiment at $273\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, the interaction between adsorbents and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is relatively higher than that of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ . It can be seen from Fig. 5A that all adsorbents can achieve efficient separations for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ (50:50, v/v) mixture gas. The ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ broke through in about 10 minutes and quickly reached saturation. $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ first broke through the column of Ni-gallate pellet, then the Mg-gallate column. The best-performing Co-gallate began to broke through until more than 80 minutes. The concentration of ethylene at the outlet was below the detection limit of the device $(1\mathrm{ppm})$ , indicating that the ethylene component has been completely removed by the pellets, which ensures that the purity of ethane reached a pure grade. Moreover, cycling breakthrough experiments with a binary mixture of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ (50:50, v/v) at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar were conducted to investigate the renewability of pellets under multiple adsorption/desorption operations. Taking Mggallate for example, continuous cycles of breakthrough experiments were conducted, as shown in Fig. 5b. The breakthrough time of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ in five cycles ranged from 100 to $120\mathrm{min}$ , whereas ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ got saturated before $30\mathrm{min}$ . There was no evident decrease in the residence times for both $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ within five cycles, which revealed the excellent regeneration capability of M-gallate pellets.
Meanwhile, the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\left(1{:}99,\mathrm{v/v}\right)$ mixture was also achieved. $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ broke through at 5 min (Fig. 5c), while the break-through point of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ delayed to 27 min (Co-gallate), 57 min $\mathbf{Mg}$ -gallate), 71 min (Ni-gallate) and reached saturation slowly. Ni-gallate pellets, the smallest pore size among the studied materials, could block the ethylene molecules on the premise of high acetylene uptake. In accordance with the highest selectivity of Ni-gallate, it exhibited the longest retention time of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the fixed bed, followed by $\mathbf{Mg}$ -gallate and Co-gallate. The presence of the HPC binder tightly intertwines the M-gallate crystals, which might hinder the internal diffusion inside the granulates, resulting in an increase of mass transfer resistance in the column and fluctuations in the breakthrough curves. Nevertheless, the obvious discrepancy between breakthrough times of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbon proved the feasibility of M-gallate pellets for practical industrial application of efficient separations for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}\left(50{:}50,\mathrm{v/v}\right)$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (1:99, v/v) mixture gas. These results revealed that M-gallate pellets can realize efficient separations for both mixtures and retain structural stability after repetitive breakthrough cycles.
