## 4.  Conclusion
In this work, we have successfully shaped three M-gallate (M=Mg, Co, Ni) materials into pellets with $95.2\%$ loading using hydroxypropyl cellulose as the binder, which performed good compatibility with M-gallate MOFs. The potential of shaped pellet for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ separation in industrial applications was validated by singlecomponent adsorption isotherms and breakthrough experiments. The $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbon adsorption results revealed that the uptake capacity of well-shaped pellets is hardly influenced after molding. Furthermore, the pellets displayed good separation performance for both $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ mixtures. In the meanwhile, multiple breakthrough experiments proved the excellent regeneration capability and structural stability of shaped M-gallate. Comparatively, the adsorption capacity and adsorption selectivity of M-gallate pellets were apparently higher than that of commercial zeolite 13X and zeolite 5A. It is worth noting that the M-gallate MOFs are readily synthesized from inexpensive gallic acid with the advantage of low cost. From a practical perspective, our shaping approach was beneficial for MOFs to realize industrial adsorption applications, thereby providing effective solutions for hydrocarbon separations. Further studies are underway on binder selection and shaping methods to optimize the structural integrity and performance of shaped pellets.
